ere a country can signal its  leadership in lots of different ways  including in sustainability  and what about more generally as as  climate change  takes hold  places that normally saw lots of snow  suddenly are seeing less snow could this  be an end for winter sports or  traditional winter sports  yeah well i think it depends on how you  define traditional i don't see the  winter olympics going away uh but i do  think  that beijing is a good uh indicator and  a good test run of how future olympics  will be uh because um sorry to interrupt  you tim but just what about actual uh  ski resorts so i'm thinking of you know  the alps or of course in canada or in  the us  yeah well certainly so the olympics will  always be able to put on events like  this they can rely and they can pay for  um artificial snow making but if you  listen to the athletes talking about um  you know their own  experiences on the slopes they're not  just thinking about themselves they're  thinking about future generations and  the health of outdoor and winter and  snow sports  in in the future so  i there is a  serious concern about the impact that  climate change is having on the ability  of you know local resorts or  just general you know mountain slopes  um  keeping  maintaining high levels of participation  which is necessary for the for the  health of the sport and and health of um  you know folks as well absolutely tim  great to get your thoughts thanks so  much tim kellerson there uh from georgia  state university  well finding replacements for plastics  in all walks of life will reduce both  pollution and our reliance on the  petrochemicals needed for production uh  in the latest of our series of climate  diaries uh we hear from notpla a company  making sustainable packaging from  seaweed  knobler is a sustainable packaging  startup we make alternative materials  out of seaweed and plants we hope to  replace single-use plastics and other  non-sustainable materials with our  products in 