rgy demands  well what we're looking at here is a  failure by the treasury to invest in in  renewable base load over the last 10  years and in insulation so it's actually  left householders vulnerable to  exploitation by the fossil fuel industry  at the moment and secondly it's less  than national grid vulnerable to  blackmail by the fossil fuel industry at  the moment um  wind electricity is now costing 40  pounds per megawatt it's 200 pounds for  for gas megawatts so what we need to be  doing is investing in green green  hydrogen plants  tidal tidal power and storage the  failure to invest in these type of gen  um electricity storage of the last 10  years have left us vulnerable to this  exploitation however there is some good  news because actually wind is now so  cheap it's actually i'm going to save  households three-quarter billion this  year and an estimated according to ssc  over the next five years something like  nine billion pounds going back to  consumers because of of the expansion in  wind energy  that's i mean that's interesting because  you're saying that there is still the  scope for renewables to fill that gap  vicki price would you agree there or do  you think we do need to fall back on uh  the traditional uh fossil fuels  well the problem is going to take quite  some time for renewables to really fill  the gap completely i mean there have  been days  recently where renewables um were not  making up very large part of the  electricity that was being used so with  gas perhaps in accounting for over half  of what we're using so that's a serious  problem so you need storage and that  storage is difficult to come by i agree  that we haven't invested enough in it  there are all sorts of ways in which  renewables can be stored but they cost  and they require quite a lot of  technologies which for some reason we  haven't necessarily invested in but also  they require some rather big grid  batteries  and again the investment needs to go in  there but until that happens we are  going to be relying quite significantly  on those gas-fired power stations  sometimes even coal fired power stations  to ensure that at least electricity is  not interrupted because unfortunately  what happens with renewables is that  they're very intermittent they you can  suddenly have new wind and the sun will  not shine what do you do then  i mean donica what do you what do you  make of that do you think you know for  you do you think this could actually be  an opportunity for us to move towards uh  investment in renewables but but even  more widely on this idea of the base  load do we need such a high base load  very good question i mean one of the in  my job as an eco auditor i visit  businesses charities homes where we are  heating lighting and cooling  empty rooms empty spaces what we really  need to question as you rightly raise is  why are we such a high base load nobody  talks about that the actual cost in  terms of putting an infrastructure to  cover the base load is extraordinarily  expensive in terms of infrastructure  investment however every kilowatt of  electricity not used is free and save us  to save us all so what i think we need  to be doing is a major program of energy  efficiency not just in um  in infrastructure and in installation  but actually in practice we need to  train the public and our businesses how  to use energy efficiently we must not we  must stop leaving the air conditioning  and heating on overnight in our in our  homes in our businesses and in our in  our tower blocks how could we do that do  you have any sort of practical solutions  as to how we could better manage that  well a timer  i have uh i've gone to charities and  they've left the the heating on all week  all night all weekend and just a simple  timer can cut their um their carbon  emissions for heating by around 40 to 50  percent a simple timer can actually be  really effective and i would ask any any  viewer who's actually at work make sure  t